      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(slack): slash replies fail with SecretRef tokens",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nFixes an issue where users invoking Slack slash commands would receive a generic failure response when the Slack account tokens were configured through SecretRefs.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nSlack slash handling now keeps the monitor's matching source-config identity when selecting refreshed runtime config. That preserves resolved secret values, rejects unrelated snapshots, and still adopts compatible routing/session refreshes between invocations.\n\nAn explicitly configured slash command now takes precedence over native Slack command mode. The two modes remain mutually exclusive, so enabling a tenant command such as `/claw` does not register an additional native-command surface.\n\n## User Impact\n\nSlack slash commands can reply normally when bot and app tokens are supplied through SecretRefs, instead of failing after the command reaches the gateway.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Added regression coverage for an unrelated runtime snapshot containing unresolved Slack token SecretRefs while the monitor holds resolved tokens.\n- Added regression coverage for matching runtime refreshes after monitor secrets have been resolved.\n- Added regression coverage that configured and native slash-command modes remain mutually exclusive.\n- Redacted live Slack proof from a SecretRef-backed Socket Mode staging tenant:\n  - Before: `/claw status` returned Slack's generic \"app did not respond\" failure.\n  - After: `/claw status` returned an ephemeral operational status response, including `OpenClaw 2026.7.2 (92924d0)`.\n  - After: `/claw help` returned the ephemeral help menu.\n  - Gateway evidence: Socket Mode connected; the after-fix commands produced no timeout or unresolved-SecretRef error.\n",
        "branch": "fix/slack-slash-secret-refs",
